    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_1a3ecfac7572b6f6c7c4c5321084d4039c1b499e21a2adfa228f2e86108275ee",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The generic U(1)^c high-temperature equation and the exceptional c=2 finite-part equation retain the same zero-location-to-exponent map even though their leading asymptotics differ. Once the Cardy-like power terms, or the c=2 T log T and linear terms, are removed, RH places every log-periodic residue contribution on the common envelope T^(c/2-3/4); in the c=2 SU(3)_1 example this becomes a T^(1/4) envelope whose low oscillation frequencies can be recovered from scalar spectral data.",
